#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa3dc44f-86e9-58c8-96da-3f517ff52007" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070f7b7d-4de6-59d3-8631-f2f1dd96090f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344231} {:answer/id #uuid "ac513e85-3ea0-5377-8564-d9e202173bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344232} {:answer/id #uuid "93887add-252d-5b7d-ae86-15a575f894eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+xy+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344233} {:answer/id #uuid "7456e408-cfa2-5ab0-8417-8be0c717e02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344234} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea23d3f-5ab8-533a-976a-1c4db5d09553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7456e408-cfa2-5ab0-8417-8be0c717e02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344234} :answer-field/id #uuid "780a5649-9207-53ae-9fbc-ca4e87b29e38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26712122-d955-52e5-a48d-0c18c4785850" :question/math-academy-id "q-161495" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+xy}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=1+xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=-2+4=2.\n$$"}]}
